<Base title="How this site teaches" wide={false}>
  <div class="eyebrow">About</div>
  <h1>How this site teaches</h1>
  <p>Every feature here is based on evidence about how people learn hard technical material.</p>
  <h2>Practice testing and spacing</h2>
  <p>Dunlosky et al.'s review of ten study techniques (<em>Psychological Science in the Public Interest</em>, 2013) rated <strong>practice testing</strong> and <strong>distributed practice</strong> highest, and rereading and highlighting low. Hence the flashcards, the check questions inside every lecture, and the scheduler that spaces reviews out.</p>
  <h2>FSRS scheduling</h2>
  <p>Cards are scheduled with FSRS (Free Spaced Repetition Scheduler), the algorithm now built into Anki, trained on hundreds of millions of real reviews. Two buttons only, Forgot and Remembered, because honest yes/no grading is faster and just as effective.</p>
  <h2>Interleaving</h2>
  <p>Mixing problem types forces you to pick the right method, which is what exams test. In Rohrer and colleagues' classroom studies, interleaved practice gave much higher test scores than blocked practice (in one study 72% vs 38%). The mixed quiz is built this way.</p>
  <h2>Pretesting ("predict first")</h2>
  <p>Trying to answer before you're taught improves learning even when your guess is wrong, as long as you see the answer afterwards (meta-analyses find moderate effects, around g ≈ 0.5).</p>
  <h2>Self-explanation and fading worked examples</h2>
  <p>Explaining steps in your own words is one of the strongest techniques (Bisra et al. 2018, g ≈ 0.55). Fully worked examples help beginners most and help less as you improve (the expertise-reversal effect), so the Guidance switch fades the working: Full → Some → Exam mode.</p>
  <h2>Pictures next to words, with signals</h2>
  <p>Mayer's multimedia principles: put words next to the picture they describe, and highlight what matters. Hence α in purple and β in teal everywhere, and every interactive saying what to notice.</p>
  <h2>Embedded review: the mnemonic medium</h2>
  <p>Andy Matuschak and Michael Nielsen's <a href="https://quantum.country">Quantum Country</a> puts spaced-repetition questions inside an explanatory essay. The "Lock it in" cards copy that idea.</p>
  <h2>Explorables and katas</h2>
  <p>Interactive simulations work best with light guidance (the PhET team's "implicit scaffolding"; Bret Victor's explorable explanations). Coding practice uses unit tests with instant feedback, like Microsoft's Quantum Katas.</p>
</Base>
